[1mThe bad path prints nothing.[22m [38;5;10mhelperBad[0m returns the address of a local [38;5;10mchar[][0m — undefined behavior, so there's no "correct" value to reproduce, only what the C actually does. GCC diagnoses it ([38;5;10m-Wreturn-local-addr[0m) and folds the return to a null pointer; the emitted function is literally [38;5;10mmov $0x0,%eax; ret[0m at both [38;5;10m-O0[0m and [38;5;10m-O2[0m. [38;5;10mprintLine[0m's NULL guard then fires, so [38;5;10mbad()[0m emits zero bytes — not even a newline. Modeling [38;5;10mhelper_bad[0m as returning [38;5;10mNone[0m reproduces that exactly while keeping the code free of dangling references. The defect is preserved, not fixed.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m truncates through [38;5;10mint*[0m.[22m glibc accumulates with [38;5;10mstrtol[0m semantics (saturating at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m) and then stores the low 32 bits. That's observable in branch selection: [38;5;10m4294967296[0m truncates to [38;5;10m0[0m and takes the bad path, while [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m saturates to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m → [38;5;10m-1[0m and takes the good path. I confirmed both against the reference binary. On conversion or input failure [38;5;10mx[0m keeps its initializer [38;5;10m0[0m, so malformed and empty input also take the bad path.[0m[0m
